00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:03.540
RON HARNIK: So Scalr is a
policy-driven cloud management

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:04.340
platform.

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:07.310
So it means that,
for example, you

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:10.290
have multiple different
clouds in your organization,

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:13.980
Scalr creates an environment
where end users can

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:16.555
operate freely out of
a self-service catalog,

00:12:16.555 --> 00:12:18.180
just pick and choose
their applications

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:19.760
and deploy their applications.

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:22.050
And Scalr takes care
of all the policy

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:23.380
that needs to be around that.

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:26.600
So, for example, you have
Team A and Team B. Team

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:29.700
A needs to be able to
operate on a large budget

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:33.040
and consume infrastructure
templates that they create,

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:35.480
and whatever size of virtual
machine that they need.

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:38.640
And Team B needs to have
access to the same GC

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:40.960
account, GC project,
for example,

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:42.110
but on a smaller budget.

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:45.290
And they can only provision
smaller-sized virtual machines.

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:48.560
And so Scalr takes care of all
of that policy around that,

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:50.600
gives you single sign-on
for multiple clouds--

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:55.230
GCE, AWS, all the
major cloud platforms.

