00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:11.370
So recently, back in
November at Dockercon,

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:13.780
we basically released what
was called Stacksmith.

00:16:13.780 --> 00:16:18.860
And Stacksmith was designed to
address issues with how do I

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:20.100
update containers?

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:22.070
And how do I do
it in a way that's

00:16:22.070 --> 00:16:24.930
capable of scaling,
capable of being automated,

00:16:24.930 --> 00:16:28.050
and capable of giving me
a notification that lets

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:29.410
me know when I need to do this?

00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:34.086
So this is kind of like your
basic lifecycle of a container.

00:16:34.086 --> 00:16:36.890
And what Stacksmith
does, it allows

00:16:36.890 --> 00:16:38.726
you to generate a
base container image.

00:16:38.726 --> 00:16:40.600
And then once it gets
into production, what's

00:16:40.600 --> 00:16:42.810
going to happen is
that you're going

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:45.171
to get a notification that
Stacksmith has an update.

00:16:45.171 --> 00:16:47.170
And we can do this via--
you want me to hold it?

00:16:47.170 --> 00:16:48.120
KIT MERKER: No, no, I got it.

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:48.760
Not now.

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:49.288
DAVID: OK.

00:16:49.288 --> 00:16:50.746
You're going to
get a notification.

00:16:50.746 --> 00:16:53.780
And this can be via a webhook,
we can give you a slack,

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:55.057
you get an email.

00:16:55.057 --> 00:16:57.640
And if you want to automate at
this point and send the webhook

00:16:57.640 --> 00:16:59.460
over here-- in this
case, to Jenkins--

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:00.670
BRAD ABRAMS: And
rebuild your solution.

00:17:00.670 --> 00:17:01.980
DAVID: Rebuild your
solution, and boom,

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:03.290
it's going to deploy
through Kubernetes.

00:17:03.290 --> 00:17:05.831
And we get the rolling updates,
and then bam, it's out there.

00:17:05.831 --> 00:17:08.759
So this is an automated
no-op scenario that's taking

00:17:08.759 --> 00:17:11.050
the stuff that Kubernetes
already does with the rolling

00:17:11.050 --> 00:17:15.560
updates, and adding the ability
to just basically do auto new

00:17:15.560 --> 00:17:17.089
container creation instead of--

00:17:17.089 --> 00:17:17.910
BRAD ABRAMS: This
helps your software

00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:20.765
be more live because there's a
dependency change or whatnot,

00:17:20.765 --> 00:17:21.890
you help pick that up and--

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:22.390
DAVID: Yeah.

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:24.320
And on top of that, this
becomes a real issue

00:17:24.320 --> 00:17:26.069
once you start to get
an environment where

00:17:26.069 --> 00:17:27.974
